1
00:00:00,014 --> 00:00:10,820
In the last two weeks, a single developer in Atlanta published a free version of Photoshop, then Illustrator, Premiere, Lightroom, After Effects, InDesign, and Acrobat.

2
00:00:11,310 --> 00:00:16,740
Then he went after Microsoft with a Word clone, an Excel clone, and a PowerPoint clone.

3
00:00:17,102 --> 00:00:20,036
And he threw in AutoCAD and Pro Tools for good measure.

4
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:24,068
All of it between September 30th and October 7th.

5
00:00:24,590 --> 00:00:38,980
And according to the project's own roadmap, getting the Word clone to full parity with Microsoft's version, a product with decades of work behind it, would take somewhere around 120 to 150 hours of AI time.

6
00:00:39,534 --> 00:00:49,220
The headline everyone ran with was that he vibe-coded the whole thing, and he even claims he one-shotted Photoshop, meaning one prompt and done.

7
00:00:49,774 --> 00:00:56,324
But when you look at how it was actually built, that story falls apart in a way that's far more interesting than the hype.

8
00:00:56,910 --> 00:01:06,320
Because the apps that work best weren't built on vibes at all, and the ones that wobble tell you exactly where the limits of this technology really are.

9
00:01:06,830 --> 00:01:11,972
Let's start with who did this and why, because the motivation is oddly relatable.

10
00:01:12,398 --> 00:01:20,196
The developer is Brandon Thomas, who runs a small studio called Artcraft, and by most accounts, it's mostly just him.

11
00:01:20,750 --> 00:01:28,132
He says the project began after he got stung by Adobe's subscription cancellation fees one too many times.

12
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:32,452
So he did something that would have sounded insane three years ago.

13
00:01:32,782 --> 00:01:47,620
He pointed Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5 at the problem, wrote the apps in the programming language Rust, and started rebuilding Adobe's lineup from scratch, publishing every one of them as open source on GitHub.

14
00:01:48,110 --> 00:01:52,068
The Git history lists Claude as a co-author on most of the commits.

15
00:01:52,846 --> 00:01:56,580
And that fee has a bigger backstory than one annoyed customer.

16
00:01:56,910 --> 00:02:13,380
In 2024, the Justice Department and the Federal Trade Commission sued Adobe, accusing it of hiding early termination fees, which, on the plan the government says Adobe steered people toward, could amount to half of your remaining payments if you canceled in the first year.

17
00:02:13,870 --> 00:02:24,420
Earlier this year, Adobe settled for $150 million, with part of it going to free services for some customers, while saying it didn't break any laws.

18
00:02:25,038 --> 00:02:27,360
So these clones didn't appear in a vacuum.

19
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:34,400
They appeared in the middle of a pricing fight where regulators had already accused the company of making it hard to leave.

20
00:02:34,702 --> 00:02:35,760
So how good are they?

21
00:02:36,046 --> 00:02:38,052
Honestly, it depends which one you ask.

22
00:02:38,446 --> 00:02:47,524
Photocraft, the Photoshop clone, handles layers, masks, adjustment layers, and a brush engine, and it opens and saves real Photoshop files.

23
00:02:47,950 --> 00:02:49,508
It's labeled Early Alpha.

24
00:02:49,838 --> 00:02:55,812
One outlet called it glitchy, and it has no support for raw photos and gaps in its AI features.

25
00:02:56,430 --> 00:03:06,564
On the Microsoft side, the register tried the Word clone on a surface laptop and said it worked pretty well, with its own buttons and icons rather than copies of Microsoft's.

26
00:03:06,990 --> 00:03:12,036
It has the ribbon, styles, tables, track changes, and mail merge.

27
00:03:12,398 --> 00:03:16,240
So these things are real, usable ish, and unfinished.

28
00:03:16,494 --> 00:03:19,044
But there's a difference between the apps that jumped out at me.

29
00:03:19,374 --> 00:03:29,252
The Photoshop clone has been put through an enormous amount of checking, while the Word clone's own roadmap admits that nobody has tested it on real documents yet.

30
00:03:29,838 --> 00:03:31,920
That difference is the whole story.

31
00:03:32,398 --> 00:03:38,638
Let's deal with the one-shot claim first, because it's the part that spread fastest and the part that doesn't hold up.

32
00:03:38,990 --> 00:03:43,856
One-shotting means giving an AI a single prompt and getting a finished product back.

33
00:03:44,206 --> 00:03:49,536
If that were what happened here, you'd expect a giant pile of code that nobody has verified.

34
00:03:49,710 --> 00:04:03,140
Instead, Photocraft has more than 1700 automated tests, and its builders checked that opening and saving Photoshop files doesn't corrupt them by running the code against large collections of real files.

35
00:04:03,662 --> 00:04:08,580
That isn't a vibe, that's about the least vibe-like thing you can do in software.

36
00:04:08,896 --> 00:04:10,020
And that's the twist.

37
00:04:10,382 --> 00:04:15,652
The apps that work weren't built by an AI freewheeling its way to something that felt right.

38
00:04:15,936 --> 00:04:19,236
They were built by an AI that was being graded constantly.

39
00:04:19,886 --> 00:04:22,176
Here's what that grading looks like in practice.

40
00:04:22,542 --> 00:04:25,220
The AI writes some code, then runs the tests.

41
00:04:25,710 --> 00:04:26,756
Hundreds fail.

42
00:04:26,990 --> 00:04:40,816
It reads exactly which ones failed and why, changes the code, and runs them again, then again and again, thousands of cycles, without ever getting bored or tired or deciding it's good enough.

43
00:04:41,166 --> 00:04:43,940
It's a lot like tuning a guitar with a clip-on tuner.

44
00:04:44,206 --> 00:04:49,028
You don't need to understand music, you just keep adjusting until the needle sits in the middle.

45
00:04:49,422 --> 00:04:54,596
The AI doesn't have to know what Photoshop is for, it has to turn red into green.

46
00:04:54,990 --> 00:05:02,020
And as long as the tests are good, that process drags the code towards something that really behaves like the original.

47
00:05:02,510 --> 00:05:05,936
Which also means the whole thing is only as good as the tests.

48
00:05:06,096 --> 00:05:12,016
If the checks are shallow, the result is shallow, and a test that nobody wrote is invisible.

49
00:05:12,256 --> 00:05:13,536
Here's why that matters.

50
00:05:13,776 --> 00:05:16,576
Picture two students preparing for the same exam.

51
00:05:16,736 --> 00:05:24,228
The first writes practice answers and then checks every one against a complete marking scheme, instantly, thousands of times.

52
00:05:24,814 --> 00:05:28,900
The second writes answers and never finds out whether any of them were right.

53
00:05:29,358 --> 00:05:33,696
Same intelligence, same effort, and you already know who you'd bet on.

54
00:05:33,998 --> 00:05:36,896
A coding AI with a test suite is the first student.

55
00:05:37,166 --> 00:05:41,456
A coding AI told to make an app that feels like Photoshop is the second.

56
00:05:41,616 --> 00:05:44,452
And that explains something that looked like a coincidence.

57
00:05:45,102 --> 00:05:49,028
Why were the first big AI-built apps all clones?

58
00:05:49,614 --> 00:05:51,300
Why not wild new inventions?

59
00:05:51,918 --> 00:05:54,052
Because cloning is easy mode.

60
00:05:54,478 --> 00:05:58,852
When you're recreating existing software, the answer key already exists.

61
00:05:59,470 --> 00:06:06,660
File formats like Photoshops and Words are publicly documented, the real apps are sitting right there to compare against.

62
00:06:07,118 --> 00:06:12,004
And the word clones makers say it was built from public specifications and observation.

63
00:06:12,430 --> 00:06:16,868
Even its spelling and hyphenation data comes from a public domain word list.

64
00:06:17,456 --> 00:06:19,172
Every attempt can be checked.

65
00:06:19,694 --> 00:06:20,896
Does this file open?

66
00:06:21,102 --> 00:06:21,936
Does it save?

67
00:06:22,222 --> 00:06:24,176
Does it match what the original does?

68
00:06:24,416 --> 00:06:33,216
When you're building something new, nobody has written the answer key, and the AI has nothing to be graded against except your opinion.

69
00:06:33,582 --> 00:06:35,216
So where does it fall apart?

70
00:06:35,502 --> 00:06:37,988
Where the answer key goes missing.

71
00:06:38,446 --> 00:06:39,536
Take the Word clone.

72
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:46,756
Artcraft's own estimate is that it's at about 62% of real feature parity, and it's still pre-alpha.

73
00:06:47,022 --> 00:06:51,076
It drops charts, smart art, and embedded objects when you save.

74
00:06:51,534 --> 00:06:59,748
And the line in its roadmap that matters most is the admission that the round trip hasn't been tested on a collection of real documents.

75
00:07:00,238 --> 00:07:02,756
In other words, it hasn't been graded yet.

76
00:07:03,214 --> 00:07:12,708
And put the speed in perspective, the roadmap estimates around 20 to 25 hours of AI work to reach a first usable alpha of Word.

77
00:07:13,006 --> 00:07:17,136
And Microsoft Word first shipped in 1983.

78
00:07:17,646 --> 00:07:20,004
A fast start isn't a finish line.

79
00:07:20,462 --> 00:07:23,716
The first stretch of a product like this is the visible part.

80
00:07:23,982 --> 00:07:33,092
And the last stretch comes from decades of customers hitting strange situations, much of which was never written down anywhere as a spec.

81
00:07:33,934 --> 00:07:43,428
The Excel clone has the same problem, because a spreadsheet isn't just math, it's years of quirks that existing workbooks quietly depend on.

82
00:07:43,854 --> 00:07:50,020
And one review noted that whether the clone reproduces those quirks is simply unconfirmed.

83
00:07:50,336 --> 00:07:52,736
Macros written in Excel scripting language.

84
00:07:53,296 --> 00:07:54,724
Won't run on it at all.

85
00:07:55,022 --> 00:07:58,404
And the Photoshop clone doesn't run Photoshop plugins.

86
00:07:59,086 --> 00:08:02,340
Wherever there's a spec and a score, it's impressive.

87
00:08:02,766 --> 00:08:04,932
Wherever there isn't, it's guesswork.

88
00:08:05,550 --> 00:08:09,252
And the evidence from outside this project points the same way.

89
00:08:09,860 --> 00:08:18,576
Veracode tested AI-generated code for security and found that about 45% of samples contain serious vulnerabilities.

90
00:08:18,798 --> 00:08:27,812
And that number hasn't improved across multiple rounds of testing, even while the same models got better at writing code that simply works.

91
00:08:28,256 --> 00:08:42,660
Research on large companies found that developers using AI commit code three to four times faster than their peers, but introduce security findings at 10 times the rate, faster and more dangerous.

92
00:08:43,182 --> 00:08:48,896
So the problem isn't AI written code, the problem is unchecked AI written code.

93
00:08:49,326 --> 00:08:53,156
If this is changing how you see AI coding, hit subscribe.

94
00:08:53,536 --> 00:08:58,896
Because next, I want to show you the part of this story that nobody has been able to explain.

95
00:08:59,056 --> 00:09:09,156
In July 2025, in a widely reported incident, an AI coding agent from Replit deleted a company's entire production database in the middle of a code freeze.

96
00:09:09,870 --> 00:09:15,556
And when it was asked about it, the AI itself said it had made a catastrophic error in judgment.

97
00:09:16,334 --> 00:09:18,628
Replit's CEO apologized publicly.

98
00:09:19,246 --> 00:09:20,932
That wasn't a failure of intelligence.

99
00:09:21,422 --> 00:09:23,044
It was a failure of guardrails.

100
00:09:23,758 --> 00:09:27,940
Nothing stopped the AI from doing something that nobody should have allowed.

101
00:09:28,686 --> 00:09:33,156
Compare that with a project where every change is graded by thousands of tests.

102
00:09:33,710 --> 00:09:37,412
It's the same kind of technology with completely different outcomes.

103
00:09:38,222 --> 00:09:40,356
Now for the part with actual drama.

104
00:09:40,686 --> 00:09:42,020
Is any of this legal?

105
00:10:01,710 --> 00:10:06,084
But he also warned that this situation could quite possibly end up in court.

106
00:10:06,542 --> 00:10:10,980
Because the company with a grievance has deep pockets and a lot to lose.

107
00:10:11,502 --> 00:10:13,284
And that raises an obvious question.

108
00:10:13,710 --> 00:10:19,376
What does clean room even mean when the builder is an AI trained on a huge slice of the internet?

109
00:10:20,014 --> 00:10:21,860
Skeptics are already asking it.

110
00:10:22,286 --> 00:10:26,244
Microsoft acknowledged questions about the clones and said nothing more.

111
00:10:26,830 --> 00:10:28,576
Then something odd happened.

112
00:10:28,816 --> 00:10:39,364
After the Office clones appeared, the download links for WordCraft, Gridcraft, and Deckcraft disappeared from the project's website, with no statement explaining why.

113
00:10:40,110 --> 00:10:49,988
The code is still on GitHub, so it hasn't vanished, but nobody outside the project knows whether this was a legal worry, a quality worry, or a developer tidying up.

114
00:10:50,606 --> 00:10:53,216
As of this recording, nobody has said.

115
00:10:53,646 --> 00:10:57,776
So here's what I'd watch from here: whether those download links come back.

116
00:10:58,094 --> 00:11:03,908
Whether anyone tests the Word clone against a pile of real documents and publishes the results.

117
00:11:04,496 --> 00:11:08,260
Because that one experiment would tell us more than any headline.

118
00:11:08,782 --> 00:11:14,976
And whether Adobe and Microsoft respond with lawyers, with lower prices, or with nothing at all.

119
00:11:15,136 --> 00:11:18,308
Let's zoom out because there's a bigger idea hiding in here.

120
00:11:18,734 --> 00:11:22,948
Wall Street has been obsessed this year with something called the SaaS Pocalypse.

121
00:11:23,438 --> 00:11:29,056
The theory that instead of paying software subscriptions, people would soon just build their own software.

122
00:11:29,296 --> 00:11:33,924
This is the first real test of that, and the result is a split decision.

123
00:11:34,190 --> 00:11:40,612
On one side, one person with an AI got further in a week than a hobbyist team could have managed before.

124
00:11:41,070 --> 00:11:46,084
Open source projects like GIMP and Krita took many years to reach a similar place.

125
00:11:46,510 --> 00:11:47,520
And it isn't a one-off.

126
00:11:47,936 --> 00:11:56,932
A separate solo developer's free Photoshop alternative called Photon reportedly passed 20,000 users, according to a post on Reddit.

127
00:11:57,358 --> 00:12:04,016
On the other side, none of these clones can touch the things that actually keep people inside Adobe and Microsoft.

128
00:12:04,206 --> 00:12:06,916
They don't run the plugins, they don't run the macros.

129
00:12:07,342 --> 00:12:15,556
And Microsoft 365 isn't just Word and Excel, it's email, Teams, SharePoint, identity, and device management.

130
00:12:15,856 --> 00:12:19,844
A whole infrastructure that a desktop clone can't reach.

131
00:12:20,302 --> 00:12:26,564
One reviewer described it as the clones chipping away at the feature wall while the catalog wall stays standing.

132
00:12:26,990 --> 00:12:28,976
The code was never the whole moat.

133
00:12:29,136 --> 00:12:32,388
What changed is that the code part just got cheap.

134
00:12:33,262 --> 00:12:39,332
Before I give you my verdict, here's a tool for the next time you see a headline that says built with AI.

135
00:12:39,776 --> 00:12:41,056
Ask three questions.

136
00:12:41,550 --> 00:12:47,044
Is there a spec, meaning a definition of correct, that existed before the AI started?

137
00:12:47,502 --> 00:12:51,780
Is there a test suite that automatically checks the work and how big is it?

138
00:12:52,238 --> 00:12:56,420
And did a human who understands the field actually look at the results?

139
00:12:57,070 --> 00:13:02,052
If the answer to all three is yes, you're probably looking at serious engineering.

140
00:13:02,446 --> 00:13:07,332
If it's no to all three, you're looking at a demo, however polished it looks.

141
00:13:08,206 --> 00:13:10,020
So is vibe coding the future?

142
00:13:10,382 --> 00:13:11,332
Here's my read.

143
00:13:11,840 --> 00:13:27,844
Vibe coding, the original meaning, where you give in to the vibes and forget the code even exists, which is how the term's creator, Andre Carpathy, described it in early 2025, is a fantastic way to make a prototype, and a terrible way to make Photoshop.

144
00:13:28,366 --> 00:13:41,092
What actually built these apps looks much more like what veteran engineers like Ken Beck call augmented coding, where the AI writes the code, but humans insist on tests, review, and oversight.

145
00:13:41,518 --> 00:13:44,676
The AI did the typing, the tests did the judging.

146
00:13:45,134 --> 00:13:46,500
And that's the real shift.

147
00:13:46,830 --> 00:13:49,732
The scare skill is no longer writing code.

148
00:13:49,934 --> 00:13:55,812
It's knowing what correct looks like and being able to write it down in a form a machine can be graded against.

